            <div xml:id="d305">

                <pb facs="0389" n="375" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg375"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.91293/16: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-10T19:00:00+07:00">March 10, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 11—11:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>456. Minister of Information H. C. Liang informed foreign correspondents at press
                    conference March 8 that while date of trip to Yenan had not yet been set they
                    would probably leave “within a month.” Liang stated correspondents would proceed
                    from Chungking to Sian, thence to Yenan and Ninghsia and added, in reply to
                    question, itinerary might include Chinghai but not Sinkiang. At press conference
                    previous week, Liang said trip would last 5 or 6 months.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
